  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Sunday)\nIssued at 119 PM CST MON NOV 16 2026\n\nA southern-stream shortwave trough continues to move eastward\nalong the Gulf Coast. Some light rain is noted just offshore,\nwhile just mid and high level clouds are present across our\nsouthern counties with very dry air at low-levels. High pressure\ncovering much of the eastern CONUS is providing dry easterly flow\nnear the surface. Have continued to use a blend of raw guidance\nfor dew points as the NBM is running as much as 10 degrees too\nhigh in some locations. Ensemble guidance has trended further to\nthe west with a cutoff low that is expected to be left behind\nalong the northeastern Gulf Coast. In response to this, ESE 850mb\nflow will begin to advect some Atlantic moisture into our far\nsouthern counties in the 850-700mb layer beginning tonight. Weak\nisentropic lift will result in some cloudcover and perhaps some\nsprinkles or light rain in our far southeastern counties tonight\nthrough Wednesday. Latest ensemble guidance suggests slightly\nbetter chances for light rain late Wednesday night into Thursday.\nAdded some low PoPs into the forecast for this period but these\nmay need to be increased further. Any rainfall amounts will\nremain light, and there is still no significant rainfall amounts\nexpected the next 7 days. This cloudcover may also limit high\ntemperatures in some of our southern counties and keep low\ntemperatures a bit warmer there.\n\nSome low rain chances may persist into Saturday before the\nshortwave finally lifts out and causes an area of low pressure to\nlift northward off the Atlantic coastline. A cold front moves\ninto the area from the northwest by Sunday, but moisture return\nwill be limited due to the Atlantic low.\n\n32/JDavis",
